   191	- Otherwise #{w ∈ (W, 2W] prime : w ≡ a (mod t)} ≤ π(2W; t, a) ≤ 4W/(φ(t) log(2W/t)).
   192	- Divide by W/(2 log W). This gives ≤ 8 log W/(φ(t) log(2W/t)).
   193	- Now log W ≤ (s + 1/(4k) + η₀) log N ≤ log N. Since 2MV_max ≤ N^{s+1/(4k)}L^{O(1)},
   194	  log(2W/t) ≥ (η₀/2) log N − O(Λ) ≥ (η₀/3) log N. ∎
   195	(Exact toy check: `bt_ratio`, `test_brun_titchmarsh_ratio`.)
   196	
   197	**Corollary W.** Let Φ: Z/t → [0, ∞) be any function. Then, by (1.1),
   198	  E_w Φ(r₀) ≤ (C_W/φ(t)) Σ_{c ∈ (Z/t)^×} Φ(c).
   199	This holds for any (ℓ, P, g) with gcd(G(P), N) = 1. ∎
   200	
   201	This replaces the large-values problem (critic piece (ii)). The bound is uniform in the rational data,
   202	so the rational weights can be summed separately (§5, Step 5).
   203	
   204	## 4. Theorem BDH_fam (critic piece (i), all-residue, at precision L^{o(1)})
   205	
   206	**Notation.**
   207	- X* := X_F^max = C′_d MH V_max^d. It is common to all ℓ ∈ J and dominates every window below.
   208	- 𝒮* := 𝒮_B ∩ [−X*, X*] ∖ {0}, with #𝒮* ≤ 3Ψ(X*, B).
   209	- S(α) := Σ_{F∈𝒮*} e(αF).
   210	- For m ≥ 1: 𝔔(m) := #{(F, F′) ∈ 𝒮*² : F ≡ F′ (mod m)}.
   211	- X′₊ := 2MV_max/Y. For ℓ ∈ J: 𝒩_ℓ := {n ∈ Z : P⁺(|n|) ≤ B, ℓ|n| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV]}. Then |n| ≤ X′₊ on 𝒩_ℓ.
   212	
   213	**Additive Parseval [proved; exact].**
   214	  𝔔(m) = (1/m) Σ_{h mod m} |S(h/m)|² = (1/m) Σ_{m′|m} f(m′),  f(m′) := Σ_{a mod m′, (a,m′)=1} |S(a/m′)|²,
   215	since each h/m is a unique reduced fraction a/m′ with m′ | m. The term m′ = 1 is |S(0)|²/m = (#𝒮*)²/m.
   216	(Integer form via Ramanujan sums: `pair_count_ramanujan`, tested.)
   217	
   218	**Cited.**
   219	- **(LS)** The additive large sieve (Montgomery–Vaughan, J. London Math. Soc. 8 (1974); Selberg): for
   220	  any a_F supported on an interval of K integers and Q ≥ 1,
   221	    Σ_{m′≤Q} Σ_{(a,m′)=1} |Σ_F a_F e(aF/m′)|² ≤ (K + Q²) Σ|a_F|².
   222	- **(DS)** pcest Lemma DS, for every residue (non-reduced included), modulus ≤ B^{C₀}, and intervals of
   223	  length ≥ XB^{−C₀} inside [XB^{−C₀}, X].
   224	- **(Hi), (C8.16)** as in pcest §2.1:
   225	  - ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) for z₂ ≥ z₁;
   226	  - ϱ(x) = ϱ(xB^c)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)} for fixed c ≥ 0.
   227	
   228	Fix C₀ with B^{C₀} ≥ 64S²·L/(ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)) for large N. This is possible because S², 1/ϱ(X′₊) and
   229	1/ϱ(X*) are L^{O(1)}. Put R₀ := B^{C₀}.
   230	
   231	**Theorem BDH_fam [proved].** Uniformly for 1 ≤ q ≤ 2S²,
   232	  (***)  E_ℓ Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} Σ_{m′ | ℓ²|n|q, m′>1} f(m′) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}·Ψ(X′₊, B)·Ψ(X*, B)².
   233	Consequently, since #𝒩_ℓ ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊),
   234	  E_ℓ Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} 𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}·Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²/(Y q (MV)²).   (4.1)
   235	
   236	*Proof.*
   237	- **Rearrangement.** Write the left side of (***) as Σ_{m′>1} f(m′)·ν(m′), where
   238	  ν(m′) := E_ℓ #{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m′ | ℓ²|n|q}. Recall ℓ > max(B, 2S²) ≥ max(P⁺(n), q), so ℓ ∤ |n|q.
   239	  Split the m′ into three classes.
   240	- **(A) ℓ ∤ m′ and 1 < m′ ≤ R₀.**
   241	  - *Bound on f.* f(m′) ≤ Σ_{a mod m′} |S(a/m′)|² = m′ Σ_{c mod m′} N_c², with N_c := #{F ∈ 𝒮* : F ≡ c (mod m′)}.
   242	  - For each c, DS (for the positive and the reflected negative parts of [X*R₀^{−1}, X*]), together
   243	    with the trivial count ≤ X*R₀^{−1}/m′ + 1 on |F| < X*R₀^{−1}, gives N_c ≤ (X*/m′)ϱ(X*)(log N)^{O(1)}.
   244	    This uses R₀^{−1} ≤ ϱ(X*) and m′ ≤ X*ϱ(X*).
   245	  - Hence f(m′) ≤ m′·max_c N_c·#𝒮* ≤ Ψ(X*)²(log N)^{O(1)}.
   246	  - *Bound on ν.* Here m′ | |n|q. Put m₂ := gcd(m′, q) and m₁ := m′/m₂, so m₁ | n. Then
   247	    ν(m′) ≤ 2Ψ(X′₊/m₁) ≤ 2(X′₊/m₁)·2ϱ(X′₊R₀^{−1}) ≤ (Ψ(X′₊)/m₁)(log N)^{O(C₀)}, by (Hi) and (C8.16).
   248	  - The map m′ ↦ (m₁, m₂) is injective, with m₂ | q. Hence Σ_{m′≤R₀} 1/m₁ ≤ τ(q)(1 + log R₀).
   249	  - So (A) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
   250	- **(B) ℓ ∤ m′ and m′ > R₀.**
   251	  - Necessarily m′ ≤ |n|q ≤ X′₊q.
   252	  - Take dyadic blocks m′ ∈ (R, 2R], R = R₀2^i. On a block, ν(m′) ≤ #{1 ≤ |n| ≤ X′₊ : m₁ | n} ≤ 2X′₊/m₁ ≤
   253	    2X′₊q/R, since m₁ ≥ m′/q.
   254	  - By (LS) with K = 2X* + 1 and Q = 2R: Σ_{m′∈(R,2R]} f(m′) ≤ (3X* + 4R²)·3Ψ(X*).
   255	  - Summing over blocks:
   256	      (B) ≤ Σ_R (2X′₊q/R)(3X* + 4R²)·3Ψ(X*) ≤ 36X′₊qX*Ψ(X*)/R₀ + 48X′₊qΨ(X*)·(4X′₊q).
   257	  - First term: 36X′₊qX*/R₀ ≤ Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*), because R₀ ≥ 36q/(ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)).
   258	  - Second term: 192X′₊q² ≤ ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)X* holds for large N. Indeed X*/X′₊ ≥ c_d HV^{d−1}Y ≥ N^{h}, and
   259	    q²/(ϱϱ) = L^{O(1)}.
   260	  - So (B) ≤ 2Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
   261	- **(C) ℓ | m′.**
   262	  - Write m′ = ℓ^i m″, with i ∈ {1, 2} and m″ | |n|q. The prime ℓ is determined by m′: it is m′'s unique
   263	    prime factor > max(B, 2S²).
   264	  - So only one ℓ contributes to ν(m′), and ν(m′) = π_J^{−1}#{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m″ | |n|q}.
   265	  - Two bounds hold: ν(m′) ≤ π_J^{−1}·3Ψ(X′₊), and, with m″ ≥ m′/ℓ² > R/(4Y²) on a block (R, 2R],
   266	    ν(m′) ≤ π_J^{−1}·2X′₊q/m″ ≤ π_J^{−1}·8X′₊qY²/R.
   267	  - The blocks run over Y/2 ≤ R ≤ R_max := 4Y²X′₊q, which is O(log N) blocks.
   268	  - By (LS):
   269	      (C) ≤ π_J^{−1} Σ_R [3Ψ(X′₊)·3X*·3Ψ(X*) + (8X′₊qY²/R)·4R²·3Ψ(X*)]
   270	          ≤ π_J^{−1}[27 log N·Ψ(X′₊)X*Ψ(X*) + 192X′₊qY²R_maxΨ(X*)].
   271	  - First term: X*/π_J ≤ Ψ(X*)/log² N, because π_J ≥ Y/(2 log Y) = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)} while 1/ϱ(X*) = L^{O(1)}.
   272	  - Second term: π_J^{−1}·768X′₊²q²Y⁴ ≤ Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*), provided X′₊q²Y³ log Y ≤ c·ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)X*. Since
   273	    X*/X′₊ ≥ c_d HV^{d−1}Y, this reduces to Y²q² log Y ≤ c·ϱϱ·HV^{d−1}.
   274	    - In exponents: 1/k < h + (d−1)/(4k), i.e. h > (5−d)/(4k).
   275	    - This holds with polynomial margin, since h > s + 1/(4k) > 1/(2k) ≥ (5−d)/(4k) for d ≥ 3.
   276	  - So (C) ≤ 2Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
   277	- **(4.1) from (***).**
   278	  - For n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ, m := ℓ²|n|q ≥ ℓqMV/32 and φ(ℓ|n|) ≥ c ℓ|n|/log log N ≥ c′MV/log log N.
   279	  - By the Parseval identity, 𝔔(m)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ (C log log N/(Yq(MV)²))·[(#𝒮*)² + Σ_{m′|m, m′>1} f(m′)].
   280	  - Sum over n, average over ℓ, and use #𝒩_ℓ ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊) and (***). ∎
   281	
   282	*Remarks.*
   283	- **What is new, and what is not.** No equidistribution (no Siegel–Walfisz input) is needed. LD only
   284	  asks for an upper bound within L^{o(1)}, so each range can be handled crudely:
   285	  - conductors ≤ B^{C₀} = L^{O(1)} with a uniform pointwise bound (DS), for any fixed C₀;
   286	  - all larger conductors with the large sieve, whose diagonal X*·Ψ is beaten either by the sparsity
   287	    ν(m′) ≲ X′₊q/m′ of the moduli divisible by m′ (class B), or by the average over the N^{1/(2k)} primes
   288	    ℓ (class C).
   289	- **The ℓ-average is essential in (C).** For one fixed ℓ, the characters to the modulus ℓ² alone give
   290	  Σ_{a mod ℓ²} |S(a/ℓ²)|² = ℓ²·Σ_c N_c², and the trivial bound for it is X*Ψ(X*) ≫ Ψ(X*)².
   291	  - Proving the single-modulus bound Σ_c N_c² ≲ L^{o(1)}Ψ²/ℓ² for one modulus ℓ² = N^{1/k} would need
   292	    smooth numbers to be L²-equidistributed modulo one modulus of size B^{Θ(u)}.
   293	  - Unconditionally this is out of reach. For example, the primes ≤ B could all lie in a subgroup of
   294	    (Z/ℓ²)^× of index up to 1/ϱ(ℓ², B) = L^{Θ(1)}: Burgess-type bounds on the least non-residue are far
   295	    above B = ℓ^{o(1)}.
   296	  - The average over ℓ ∈ J removes this.
   297	- **The family's sparsity is used only through ν(m′) ≤ 2X′₊q/m₁.** This is a divisibility count. No
   298	  smoothness of the moduli is needed beyond (A).
   299	- **Prop B5 is not contradicted.** BDH_fam is an L² statement: all residue classes at once, weighted by
   300	  their own mass. It never passes through max-class ratios.
   301	
   302	## 5. Theorem LD* for (a‴_k)
   303	
   304	**Theorem LD* [proved].**
   305	- **Hypotheses.** Those of Theorem FM for (a‴_k) (§2), and S² = (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F) as in pcest
   306	  Theorem 4.3. Let N_ℓ(A) be the filtered count.
   307	- **Conclusion.**
   308	    E[N(N−1)] ≤ τ*(S)(log N)^{O(1)}·S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F²,  τ*(S) := max_{q≤2S²} τ(q) = L^{o(1)}.
   309	- Equivalently, in pcest's normalization (Prop 4.2 and ϱ(T|G|) ≥ ρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)} under the filter):
   310	    E_{ℓ,w} Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_{g, filtered} Y_PY_{P′}(S²/q)κ_alg ≤ L^{o(1)}·S⁴ρ_r².
   311	- With Theorem FM: PC ≤ L^{o(1)}, and D_k(γ) holds with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)} (Prop PZ).
   312	
   313	*Proof.* Write T := dHV^{d−1}, t := |G_g(P)|, t′ := |G_g(P′)| and q := |Δ|/ℓ ∈ [1, 2S²]. Sums over pairs run
   314	over ordered pairs P ≠ P′ in 𝒫_ℓ ∩ Λ_ℓ(g) with ℓ | Δ and both |G|, |G′| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV].
   315	
   316	- **Step 0.** E[N(N−1)] = E_{ℓ,g,w} Σ_{P≠P′} Y_PY_{P′}·Pr_c[F_A(P), F_A(P′) ∈ 𝒮_B].
   317	  - Pairs with ℓ ∤ Δ contribute nothing (pcest Lemma Q, step 0).
   318	  - Every quantity except F̂_{g,w} is independent of w.
   319	- **Step 1 (kernel).**
   320	  - By Lemma Kern(iii) with the effective wedge bound ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ |Δ|(V/2)^{2d−4}, Pr_c[Φ(c) = z] ≤
   321	    C_d/(H²V^{2d−4}) for every z.
   322	  - Φ(C_H) ⊂ [−T, T]², and Γ = Φ(Z^d) = {(z₁, z₂) : z₂ ≡ κ^{d−1}z₁ (mod Δ)} (Kern(i)). Hence
   323	      Pr_c[both smooth] ≤ (C_d/(H²V^{2d−4}))·Z,  Z := Σ_{j mod |Δ|} A_j(P)A_{κ^{d−1}j}(P′),
   324	    where A_j(P) := #{z ∈ [−T, T] : z ≡ j (mod |Δ|), F̂(P) + G(P)z ∈ 𝒮_B}.
   325	- **Step 2 (AM–GM).**
   326	  - κ is a unit mod Δ (P′ is primitive), so j ↦ κ^{d−1}j is a bijection of Z/|Δ|.
   327	  - Hence Z ≤ ½(Q(P) + Q(P′)), where Q(P) := Σ_j A_j(P)². Q(P) depends on (P, g, w, |Δ|) only.
   328	- **Step 3 (enlargement to a residue-only quantity).**
   329	  - z ↦ F = F̂(P) + G(P)z is injective, F ≡ r₀ (mod t), and z ≡ z′ (mod |Δ|) ⟺ F ≡ F′ (mod t|Δ|).
   330	  - Every such F lies in [−X*, X*], by §1 sizes. Hence
   331	      Q(P) ≤ 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; r₀) := #{(F, F′) ∈ 𝒮*² : F ≡ F′ ≡ r₀ (mod t), F ≡ F′ (mod t|Δ|)}.
   332	  - 𝔔₀ depends on w only through r₀ = F̂_{g,w}(P) mod t.
   333	- **Step 4 (average over w).**
   334	  - By Corollary W, E_w 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; r₀) ≤ (C_W/φ(t)) Σ_{c∈(Z/t)^×} 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; c).
   335	  - The classes c partition the pairs counted, so this is ≤ (C_W/φ(t))·𝔔(t|Δ|).
   336	  - Likewise for P′, with t′.
   337	  - The pair sum is symmetric under (P, P′) ↦ (P′, P): Δ ↦ −Δ, and the filter and the Y's are symmetric.
   338	  - Therefore
   339	      E[N(N−1)] ≤ (C_dC_W/(H²V^{2d−4}))·E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P≠P′} Y_PY_{P′}·𝔔(t ℓq)/φ(t).   (5.1)
   340	- **Step 5 (the rational weights; fibres).**
   341	  - Fix ℓ, P = (a, b) and P′ with |Δ| = ℓq. The summand of (5.1) depends on g only through n := G_g(P)/ℓ,
   342	    and Y_P = 1 ⟺ n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ.
   343	  - For fixed n, the g ∈ 𝒢 with G_g(P) = ℓn lie on the line g = g* + j·(a, b) in (g₀, g₁)-coordinates.
   344	    The direction is primitive and |(a, b)| ≥ b > V/2, so j ranges over ≤ √2M/V + 1 integers.
   345	  - Along it, G_g(P′) changes by ba′ − ab′ = −Δ per step. So n′ := G_g(P′)/ℓ runs through an arithmetic
   346	    progression of difference q, inside an interval of length A := q(√2M/V + 1).
   347	  - The filter puts |n′| ∈ [X_r/64, X_r], with X_r := 2MV/ℓ.
   348	  - So for each sign the n′-values lie in an interval I_± ⊂ [X_r/64, X_r] with |I_±| ≤ A, in a fixed
   349	    class mod q.
   350	  - Apply DS with X = X_r and modulus q ≤ 2S² ≤ B^{C₀}. Enlarge I_± to an interval of length
   351	    max(|I_±|, X_rB^{−C₀}) inside [X_rB^{−C₀}, X_r]; positivity allows this. Since X_rB^{−C₀}/q ≤ M/V,
   352	    this gives
   353	      #{g ∈ 𝒢 : G_g(P) = ℓn, Y_{P′} = 1, P′ filtered} ≤ U := (3M/V)ρ_r(log N)^{O(1)},
   354	    uniformly in ℓ, P, P′ and n.
   355	  - Hence Σ_g Y_PY_{P′}𝔔(tℓq)/φ(t) ≤ U·Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} 𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|).
   356	- **Step 6 (partners).**
   357	  - For fixed P and q, at most 12 points P′ ∈ 𝒫_ℓ have det(P, P′) = ±ℓq.
   358	  - They lie on two lines parallel to P, on which consecutive integer points differ by P, with |P| ≥ V/2.
   359	    Each line meets [−V, V]² in a segment of length ≤ 2√2V.
   360	- **Step 7 (assembly).**
   361	  - By (5.1), Steps 5–6 and (4.1), using |𝒢| ≥ cM², #𝒫_ℓ ≤ CV_max²/log V and V_max/V ≤ 2:
   362	      E[N(N−1)] ≤ C·(H²V^{2d−4})^{−1}·M^{−2}·V²·(M/V)ρ_r·Σ_{q≤2S²} τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²/(Yq(MV)²).
   363	  - Here Ψ(X′₊) ≤ (2MV/Y)ρ_r(log N)^{O(1)} and Ψ(X*) ≤ C MHV^d ρ_F(log N)^{O(1)}, by (Hi) and (C8.16), and
   364	    Σ_q τ(q)/q ≤ (log S)².
   365	  - Collecting powers:
   366	      V²·(M/V)·(MV/Y)·(MHV^d)²/(H²V^{2d−4}·M²·Y·(MV)²) = V⁴/Y² ≍ S⁴.
   367	  - Hence E[N(N−1)] ≤ τ*(S)(log N)^{O(1)}S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F².
   368	  - **The normalized form.** (S²/q)κ_alg = Z/(4d²H²V^{2d−4}ϱ(T|G|)ϱ(T|G′|)), and ϱ(T|G|) ≥ ρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)}
   369	    under the filter. Steps 2–7, applied to Z, give the displayed bound.
   370	  - **PC.** By Theorem FM, (E N)² ≥ S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F²/(log N)^{O(1)} and E N ≥ 1. Hence PC ≤ τ*(S)(log N)^{O(1)} =
   371	    L^{o(1)}, since τ(q) ≤ exp(O(log q/log log q)) and log q = O(Λ). ∎
   372	
   373	*Remarks.*
   374	- **Theorem R is not used.** The rational pair correlation is re-derived inside Step 5, by the same
   375	  divisor-switching argument, because the algebraic factor no longer depends on the fibre variable.
   376	- **No large-values input appears.** The only place where the residue enters is Step 4, and there it is
   377	  integrated out over w before any arithmetic estimate is applied.
   378	- **Uniformity.** All constants depend only on (d, k, a, the σ-range, η₀). Every "for large N" is a
   379	  polynomial margin (`src/ldbound/params.py: margins`) or an L^{O(1)} comparison.
   380	- **LD versus LD\*.** pcest's literal LD has right side L^{o(1)} × (rational pair count). We prove the
   381	  bound Theorem 4.3 consumes, L^{o(1)}S⁴ρ_r². The two agree if the rational pair count is
   382	  ≥ S⁴ρ_r²L^{−o(1)}; this lower bound is not needed and we do not prove it.
   383	
   384	## 6. Theorem A‴
   385	
   386	**Theorem A‴ [conditional on LV6; plus pcest's explicit caveats].**
   387	- **Hypotheses.** Fix odd d ≥ 3, k > (d+1)/4 and η₀ > 0, and put θ‴ := 3/(d+1) + d/(4k) + η₀ ≤ 1.
   388	  Let h := 1/(d+1) + 1/(4k) + η₀, a := θ‴/√2 and B = L[1/2, a].
   389	- Assume:
   390	  - LV6 for design (a‴_k): LV §6 adapted to pure-descent rows at height V, the non-monic pair
   391	    (f, g₁x − g₀) with Res = ±Nw, and the g₁-parity column;
   392	  - HECM's running-time theorem, as in pcest (Theorem A′ removes this assumption, at the constants of
   393	    the table);
   394	  - the LV Lemma 5.1 irreducibility transfer, at the level of statements.
   395	- **Conclusion.** Every N is factored in expected time L_N[1/2, (1+θ‴)/√2 + o(1)].
   396	- Infimum over k: (1 + 3/(d+1))/√2, i.e. d = 9: 0.9192, d = 11: 0.8839, d = 19: 0.8132.
   397	- c < 1 holds for d = 9 from k = 20 on, for d = 11 and 13 from k = 17, and for d = 7 from k = 45.
   398	
   399	*Proof.* pcest Theorem A's proof (steps 1–5) applies verbatim, with design (a‴_k):
   400	- Theorem 4.3(ii) is replaced by Theorem LD* + FM + Prop PZ;
   401	- T_SQ = S²L^{o(1)} = L^{θ‴/(2a)+o(1)}.
   402	- The new field parameter w costs O(log W) primality tests per field, i.e. (log N)^{O(1)}. ∎
   403	
   404	## 7. Design (a″_k): what remains open, and why the averaging does not suffice
   405	
   406	**Prop 7.1 (the exact missing statement for (a″_k)) [proved reduction; the statement itself open].**
   407	Steps 0–3 and 5–7 of §5 hold for (a″_k) verbatim; take w = 1, and X* of the old size. Step 4 is the only
   408	step that uses w. Hence LD* for (a″_k) follows from the following estimate, with c_P = N b^d g₁^{−d} and
   409	g₁ = g₁(j) along the fibre:
   410	
   411	    E_ℓ Σ_{P, q} Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} Σ_{j : Y_{P′}(j)=1} 𝔔₀(ℓ|n|, ℓq; c_{P,j})
   412	        ≤ L^{o(1)}·E_ℓ Σ_{P,q} Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} U·𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|).
   413	
   414	This is a *correlation* between two objects along the fibre:
   415	- the smoothness of the affine sequence n′(j) = n′₀ ∓ qj;
