   664	### 4.3 Why LD is not available, and what each known tool gives
   665	
   666	All items below are statements about what the cited tools give by themselves. They are not lower bounds
   667	on LD, and they do not exclude proofs that combine these tools with further arithmetic information.
   668	
   669	- **(B1′) Naive use of Harper's BDH (all residues, arbitrary residue) loses the factor G.** For one
   670	  modulus m = G|Δ|:
   671	  - LD needs the |Δ| lift classes, with main-term mass |Δ|(Ψ/m)² = Ψ²/(G·m).
   672	  - Harper's Theorem 2 (Q ≤ Ψ) controls Σ_{m≤Q} Σ_{all reduced r}|E(m, r)|², a global sum whose
   673	    per-modulus scale is Ψ²/m.
   674	  - Harper's asymptotic BDH for general sequences (J. London Math. Soc. 112 (2025) e70293,
   675	    arXiv 2412.19644, Cor. 1) confirms that the all-residue variance is dominated by its diagonal QΨ.
   676	    It has no restricted-moduli or restricted-residue form either.
   677	  - Positivity cannot recover the factor G = N^{s+1/(4k)+o(1)}, and e^{−cu/log²u} cannot compensate a
   678	    polynomial factor (codex pcest_1, Q2). The modulus family is also sparse, with density
   679	    ≈ ρ_r/(ℓ log Y), so restricted versions are needed anyway, as in barrier B1.
   680	- **(B2′) Divisor switching needs small moduli.** Lemma DS works because q ≤ y^{O(1)}.
   681	  - Applied to F in a progression mod G ≈ N^{s+1/(4k)}, the complementary-factor count is only
   682	    nontrivial when the divisor cutoff is D ≲ X_F/G.
   683	  - The divisor sum is then evaluated at density ϱ(X_F/(Gy)) instead of ϱ(X_F), a loss of L^{Θ(1)}.
   684	  - The q-part of the coupling (modulus q ≤ 2S²) is small. But it sits inside an AP to the large
   685	    modulus G, so the same obstruction applies.
   686	- **(B3′) The arithmetic large sieve loses 1/(ρ_Fδ) [assessment].** Use the large sieve in its standard
   687	  form, over primitive characters, or over primes p ≤ Q with Σ_p p Σ_w |Z(p, w) − Z/p|² ≤ (x + Q²)Z for
   688	  general moduli via induced-character multiplicities. It is insensitive to the sparseness of smooth
   689	  numbers.
   690	  - Its diagonal term x·Ψ exceeds the needed scale by the factor 1/(ρ_Fδ) = L^{Θ(1)}, where δ is the
   691	    density of the modulus family.
   692	  - The same phenomenon is why Fouvry–Tenenbaum's large-sieve BV works only for y ≥ exp(log^{2/3+ε}x)
   693	    (Harper's introduction).
   694	- **Prop B5 (an L¹ saving alone does not give L² control) [proved; elementary; scope narrowed after
   695	  codex pcest_2].**
   696	  - The max-residue estimates we know are L¹ bounds over moduli of β − 1, with saving
   697	    ε_N = H(u)^{−c} + …. These are Harper Thm 1, LV Lemma 8.5 and our Lemma 8.5′, and, with fixed
   698	    residues, de la Bretèche–Drappeau, Drappeau and Pascadi.
   699	  - The proof of LD from β-information needs an L² bound: κ_alg ≤ β(G|Δ)·β(G′|Δ)-type products, and
   700	    neither factor is controlled pointwise.
   701	  - Example: let β = T with probability ε_N/T, and β = 1 otherwise, with T ≤ 1/ρ_F (the trivial range).
   702	    The L¹ excess is ε_N, but the L² excess is ε_NT, which reaches ε_N/ρ_F = L^{Θ(1)}.
