   132	## 2. Cost of Dixon descent (heuristic smoothness model; implications from Hypothesis D)
   133	
   134	**One-level descent.**
   135	- *Split.* (u, v) is the Gauss-reduced shortest vector of {u ≡ Rv (mod N)}, so |u|, |v| ≤ √(2N). Accept iff u, v are
   136	  B1-smooth.
   137	- *Special-q.* For each odd-valuation prime ℓ ∈ (B, B1] of uv, search the special-q box of Λ_ℓ = {a ≡ bm (mod ℓ)}. This
   138	  needs a regular reduced basis ≍ √ℓ; for random m the irregular case, m/ℓ close to a low-height rational, is rare. The
   139	  search looks for primitive (a, b) with (a − bm)/ℓ and F(a, b) both B-smooth.
   140	- *Substitution.* Then ℓ ≡ φ(a − bα)/c with c = (a − bm)/ℓ.
   141	- *Rows and cost.* Use C·L^{o(1)} pure-descent rows; LV §6 needs extra character columns and combinations. Every nonzero
   142	  dependency then contains a descent relation. Total cost ≈ C·L^{o(1)}·(k·T_SQ + T_split) + T_LA.
   143	
   144	**(a) Fixed odd d, L[1/2] scale.**
   145	- Use a non-monic f of degree d with m = N^{s+o(1)}, ‖f‖ = N^{s+o(1)}, s = 1/(d+1). Take B = L[1/2, a] and
   146	  B1 = exp((log N)^{3/4}).
   147	- Rigorous HECM (Lenstra–Pila–Pomerance) tests B1-smoothness in L_{B1}[2/3] = exp(O((log N)^{1/2}(log log N)^{1/3})) =
   148	  L[1/2, o(1)].
   149	- The split succeeds with probability ρ(u₁)² = L^{−o(1)}, u₁ = (log N)^{1/4}/2. Each split has k ≍ (log N)^{1/4} + O(log log N)
   150	  large primes.
   151	- The ℓ^{(d−1)/2} and box factors are N^{o(1)}. So both sides have size N^{θ+o(1)}, θ = 2s, and T_SQ = L[1/2, θ/(2a)].
   152	- Optimum a = 1/√(d+1), giving **L[1/2, 2/√(d+1)]**: d = 3: 1; d = 5: 0.816; d = 7: 0.707. This is the formal constant of
   153	  plain fixed-degree NFS.
   154	- **(a′) Level-of-distribution-compatible design.**
   155	  - With LV randomization, F(a, b) ≡ N b^d (mod t) with t = a − bm. At the sizes above, t = |F|^{1−o(1)} (codex lemmaM_2),
   156	    which no BV-type theorem for smooth numbers reaches.
   157	  - Inflate the coefficients of R to H ≥ M^{2+ε}. Then t ≤ |F|^{1/2−ε}, the range of LV's own §8 analysis.
   158	  - This costs F ≍ N^{2s} and the rational cofactor ≍ N^{s}, so θ = 3/(d+1) and c = √(6/(d+1)): < 1 for odd d ≥ 7.
   159	  - The moduli are still multiples of ℓ with a moving residue N b^d. Their first moment is part of Hypothesis D and is open.
   160	
   161	**(b) One level, growing degree.**
   162	- B = L[α], log B1 ≍ (log N)^{β₁}(log log N)^{1−β₁}, d ≍ (log N/log log N)^{(1−β₁)/2}.
   163	- The split forces β₁ ≥ 1 − α. The special-q values have log-size ≍ (log N)^{(1+β₁)/2}(log log N)^{(1−β₁)/2}, which forces
   164	  β₁ ≤ 4α − 1. Hence α ≥ 2/5, with equality at β₁ = 3/5, d ≍ (log N/log log N)^{1/5}, and log-sizes
   165	  ≍ (log N)^{4/5}(log log N)^{1/5}.
   166	- HECM on B1 = L[3/5] costs L_{B1}[2/3] = L[2/5].
   167	- So L[2/5, c] for an unoptimised c. The one-stage 2/5 is in Gordon's NFS-DL analysis (1993). Only the factoring and
   168	  fruitfulness packaging is ours.
   169	- With k ≍ (log N/log log N)^{2/5}, a per-target success probability (log N)^{−O(1)} still gives L[2/5, c′].
   170	
   171	**(c) No rigorous L[1/3] from this architecture.**
   172	- Rigorous HECM at B1 = L[2/3] costs L[4/9]. More generally, HECM splitting needs 2β₁/3 ≤ α, and with 1 − β₁ ≤ α this
   173	  again forces α ≥ 2/5 (codex lemmaM_2).
   174	- One-level descent of L[2/3] primes costs L[1/2] at the re-optimised degree d ≍ (log N)^{1/6}, or L[2/3] at the L[1/3]-NFS
   175	  degree.
   176	- Multi-level descent would also make the special-q primes field-dependent. Calling this "strictly harder" is an
   177	  assessment.
   178	
   179	**(d) QS analogue (the toy of E1).**
   180	- Special-q relation x² − N = ℓ·c with x ≡ ±√N (mod ℓ), scanned near √N. The cofactor ≍ 2√N·J is independent of ℓ.
   181	- Splits are rejected if a large prime is inert in Q(√N). Example: N = 77, R = 16 ≡ 3/5, and both 3 and 5 are inert. The
   182	  rejection costs 2^{−k} = L^{−o(1)}.
   183	- Heuristic L[1/2, 1], with fruitfulness from DD.
   184	
   185	## 3. Hypothesis D and what it needs
   186	
   187	**Hypothesis D (open).** Use LV-randomized fields A = (m, f): m uniform; f = f̂_{N,m} + (x − m)Σ c_i x^{d−1−i}, c_i
   188	i.i.d. uniform. Let q(A) be the probability that a capped one-level descent of a uniform square succeeds in field A. Then
   189	Pr_A[q(A) ≥ L^{−o(1)}] ≥ L^{−o(1)}, with time cap T_D.
   190	
   191	**Corollary DD [proved implication, modulo an adaptation of LV §6 that is not written out].** Hypothesis D implies that
   192	every N is factored in expected time (C·T_D + T_LA)·L^{o(1)}.
   193	- The algorithm: good-field search with restarts, capped descents, pure-descent rows, LV's character columns and square
   194	  root, then Theorem DD.
   195	- The LV §6 adaptation covers characters, square roots and the height bounds. LV's Thm 2.6 assumes pairs of height
   196	  ≤ L[1/3]; ours have height S√ℓ.
   197	- Regime (a′): L[1/2, √(6/(d+1)) + o(1)]. Regime (b): L[2/5, c].
   198	
   199	**The three missing ingredients [assessment; codex lemmaM_2 and the critic concur].**
   200	1. **First moment at the right moduli.** E_A[q(A)] needs smooth values of F(a, b) in progressions N b^d mod t, with t = ℓc
   201	   a multiple of a fixed special-q prime, averaged over special-q boxes.
   202	   - At fixed d with minimal height, t ≍ |F|^{1−o(1)}, so no known estimate applies.
   203	   - Under (a′), the level 1/2 is respected, but the family of moduli (multiples of ℓ, moving residues) is not LV's
   204	     averaging family.
   205	   - Pascadi's 5/8 needs a fixed residue.
   206	2. **The complete-target-list average.** The needed statement is Pr_{A,Z}[N_ℓ(A) > 0 for every target ℓ of the split Z]
   207	   ≥ L^{−o(1)}, under the actual accepted-split law.
   208	   - Reverse Markov then gives good fields, so stochastic deepening *does* apply once this average is proved. An earlier
   209	     draft said otherwise; that was wrong (critic, codex).
   210	   - The marginal "Lemma SQ" of an earlier draft does not imply it. Two field types could each descend complementary
   211	     prime types (codex).
   212	   - The natural proof is a second moment of pair-correlation type, with loss exp(o((log N)^{1/4})) in (a) and polylog in
   213	     (b).
   214	3. **Split counting.** The split law has saddle weights ℓ^{−σ}, not 1/ℓ. It carries the square-class condition
   215	   u/v ∈ (Z/N)^{×2}, presumably handled by the Dixon/Pomerance class-pairing trick, but coprimality complicates it.
   216	   Large primes must land in a field-dependent good set.
   217	
   218	**Research programme, not a reduction.** Two randomized algebraic sides f_j = f̂_j + (x − m)R_j remove the deterministic
   219	rational side. Descend only primes that split completely in K₁.
   220	- The joint density of (⟨c, v⟩, ⟨c, v′⟩) is ≤ 1/(2H²‖v ∧ v′‖) for continuous cubes (Ball's slicing bound).
   221	- But the coefficients are integers, so lattice, index and congruence effects must be handled.
   222	- Arithmetic restrictions survive.
   223	- The target set becomes field-dependent.
   224	- The splitting density needs an S_d Galois group and uniform Chebotarev.
   225	
   226	**Why this is still a different problem from Conjecture F [assessment].** Conjecture F needs equidistribution of the
   227	Bockstein half of a hidden 2-power character, which is already sub-Burgess for QS and needs a least-nonresidue bound for
   228	degree 1 (§5). Hypothesis D needs smooth-value statements of LV's kind, but per-target and jointly. It needs no hidden
   229	2-adic data. It is neither shown easier nor shown harder; it moves the difficulty from character theory to multiplicative
   230	number theory.
