   232	## 4. Averaging over N (angle a)
   233	
   234	**4.1 Decoupling parametrizations [proved; elementary].**
   235	- **QS.** N = x² − s and (x/N) = (−s/x) for odd x and Blum N (codex (8)). The weight becomes 1_{P⁺(s) ≤ B}. The second
   236	  moment over N needs 1_sm(s)·1_sm(s + x′² − x²), a binary problem (codex (10)).
   237	- **LV.** For fixed m, (N, R) ↦ f is injective (codex (11)). The semiprime condition f(m) ∈ 𝓕 remains.
   238	- **Degree 1.** The determinant couples the coefficients (codex (12)).
   239	
   240	**4.2 Lengths [proved, codex lemmaM_1 (5)].**
   241	- Σ_m |Σ_t a_t(t/m)|² ≪ min(Q² + H, Q + H²)Σ|a_t|² (squarefree restrictions; absolute constant). So for Q ≥ H² there is no
   242	  ε-loss. Heath-Brown's (QH)^ε matters only near Q ≍ H.
   243	- Liu (arXiv 2505.09637; cited by codex, unverified by us) makes the ε-dependence explicit, exp₄(C/ε).
   244	- The obstruction is the N-dependence of the weights and the adaptive test w, not the ε.
   245	
   246	**4.3 Results and limits.**
   247	- **Theorem A (codex lemmaM_1 §5) [proved; checked by us and by the critic].** For fixed weights on odd t ≤ H < √N and a
   248	  balanced Blum family of size ≍ N/(log N)², the exceptional proportion is ≪ (log N)²E/(η²A²). In particular, Jacobi signs
   249	  on the odd L[1/3]-smooth t ≤ L[2/3] are balanced for almost all n.
   250	- **Theorem B (codex) [proved].** With t a uniform prime ≤ B, x(t) = (e_t, 1) and β_n(t) = [(t/n) = −1], every fixed test
   251	  cancels for almost all n, yet min_w δ_w = 0 for every n.
   252	- **Counting observation [observation].** The LV parameter space has size N^{δκ−1+o(1)}, which is ≪ 2^C. Bounds on bad
   253	  sets for each fixed test cannot be summed over tests.
   254	- **Fibre mixing (codex (28)–(30)) [proved].** δ ≥ η* := Σ_x min(Pr[x, β = 0], Pr[x, β = 1]). This is stronger than Lemma M.
   255	  On descent relations DD supplies η* = 1/2 conditionally.
   256	- **QS fixed-test first moment [sketch].** Via N = x² − s, reciprocity and (5) with moduli ≍ √X and length ≍ √X·H, one gets
   257	  Σ_N Z_w(N) = o(Σ_N |X_N|) when Hρ → ∞. This gives only an average δ_w ≥ 1/2 − o(1) for each w, which is useless for Lemma M
   258	  by Theorem B.
   259	
   260	**Verdict on (a):** refuted for this model, i.e. fixed-test averaging. A route via averaging needs an inverse theorem or
   261	another way to control the adaptive predictor. DD moves the factoring question elsewhere; it does not resolve this one.
   262	
   263	## 5. Involutions (angle b) and barriers
   264	
   265	- **Unit twist [proved, classical; E2].**
   266	  - In the continued-fraction relations of √N, k ↦ k + P is multiplication by ε = a + b√N, the fundamental unit of
   267	    Z[√N] (the Pell order).
   268	  - It preserves the Q_k and multiplies the root by a mod N, with a² ≡ (−1)^P. Signed norms change when P is odd.
   269	  - For Blum N, a is always a nontrivial √1. The argument (codex, critic): there is no negative Pell solution since some
   270	    p ≡ 3 (mod 4); if a ≡ ±1 (mod N), factoring (a − 1)(a + 1) = Nb² gives a smaller positive solution or a negative one.

