  \]
- **The \(g_1\) column:** record atomic relation multiplicities, as P773–774 already requires. There is no new \(w\)-parity column.
- **Transcript invariance:** sampling \(w\), selecting fields and performing gcd checks use the field and public square \(R\); they do not inspect its hidden root.
- **Irreducibility:** the prescribed value becomes \(Nw\); the additional excluded sieve primes divide \(w\). Rejection remains necessary because reducibility need not factor \(N\).

The sentence at D67 asserting that the square-root step uses “only” the modular root and coefficient size is too sweeping. LV §6 also supplies characters, exceptional square classes and complexity bounds. Its adaptation must remain an explicit assumption for the **new law**, including the selected-field law. The published theorem does not establish that adaptation at these heights. [Lee–Venkatesan, §§5–6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

**Theorem A‴, D389–405: CORRECT WITH FIXES as a conditional reduction.** With those conventions, its cost calculation is
\[
\max\left\{
a+\frac1{\sqrt2},\
a+\frac{\theta'''}{2a},\
2a
\right\}.
\]
At \(a=\theta'''/\sqrt2\), \(\theta'''\le1\), this is \((1+\theta''')/\sqrt2\). Prime sampling adds only polynomial time in \(\log N\). LV6, HECM and the irreducibility transfer remain assumptions.

**Props. 7.1–7.3.**

**Prop. 7.1, D409–420: CORRECT WITH FIXES as a sufficient reduction.** The displayed sum leaves \(P'\) unbound. Include a sum over its bounded collection of partners, with the filters and admissible fibre indices explicit. Step 5 cannot be used literally before the residue dependence has been removed; the proposed correlation estimate performs that removal. Call this a sufficient missing estimate. AM–GM and enlargement do not establish necessity.

**Prop. 7.2, D422–436: WRONG as a proved impossibility statement.** Conditional determinism does not prohibit averaging over the variables that determine the residue while retaining their weights. A deterministic function of random data can be uniformly distributed, including after conditioning on another function of those data.

Dropping \(Y_{P'}\) loses one \(\rho_r\) in the particular crude upper-bound calculation. That is not an exact loss theorem. The assertion “No estimate on the residue side can recover this loss” is unsupported. Replace the proposition by an account of what the present estimates fail to prove.

**Prop. 7.3, D438–456: CORRECT WITH FIXES within the fixed-\(M\), uniform-envelope method.** The indicator argument gives the support barrier. The integer-radius bound needs an immaterial additive \(1\). The coefficient and norm-equivalence arguments then force \(h\ge s+\nu-o(1)\), provided the design retains \(M=\lfloor N^s\rfloor\) and a uniform whole-box bound \(X_F=O(MHV^d)\).

Its claimed cost relative to the old design is wrong when the old kernel floor binds. The actual increase is
\[
\Delta\theta
=s+\nu-\max\{2\nu,s-(d-2)\nu\}
=\min\{s-\nu,(d-1)\nu\}.
\]
The advertised \((d-1)\nu\) applies only in the appropriate large-\(k\) regime.

There is also a cheaper design once \(M\) is allowed to change. Put \(M=N^m\). The sharper GB estimate is
\[
\|\widehat f\|_\infty\ll_d Nw/M^d+M.
\]
For a prime randomizer of exponent \(m+\nu\), the relevant height requirements become
\[
m>\nu,\qquad
h>\max\{2\nu,\ m-(d-2)\nu,\ 1-dm+\nu\},
\]
with suitable slack. The resulting exponent is
\[
\theta=2m+h+(d-1)\nu.
\]
The BDH conditions continue to hold.

For example, at \(d=9,k=30\), take
\[
m=\frac8{75},\qquad h=\frac{29}{600}+\eta.
\]
The window and FM level conditions have positive slack, and
\[
\theta=\frac{197}{600}+\eta,\qquad
c=0.93927\ldots+O(\eta),
\]
versus \(0.97227\ldots\) for the stated design. This variation requires its corresponding LV6 assumption. It shows why the barrier’s fixed-\(M\) scope matters.

Even with fixed \(M\), the fixed polynomial slack is avoidable: choose \(W\) above \(2MV_{\max}\) by a polylogarithmic factor and \(H/W\) by a sufficiently large fixed power of \(L\). Brun–Titchmarsh then costs a polylogarithm and FM’s errors remain absorbable. This reaches \(h=s+\nu+o(1)\); it does not lower the leading height exponent.

**Constants table, D69–82: CORRECT WITH FIXES.**

All five main rows and both minimum-\(k\) columns match independent recomputation. They are zero-slack constants. The minimum-\(k\) assertions mean that sufficiently small positive slack can be chosen. For example, \(d=9,k=20\) requires
\[
\eta _0<\sqrt2-1-\frac{33}{80}
=0.001713562\ldots .
\]
The general restriction \(\eta _0<1/(8k)\) alone does not guarantee \(c<1\).

The infimum requires letting the slack tend to zero as well as taking arbitrarily large fixed \(k\).

D81’s Pollard–Strassen number is wrong. For \(d=11\),
\[
c_{\rm PS}(100)=0.9425145\ldots,\qquad
c_{\rm PS}(1000)=0.9169712\ldots,\qquad
\lim c_{\rm PS}=0.9142136\ldots .
\]
Delete “\(0.9033\to0.9142\).” A fully HECM-free claim also requires the assumed LV6 implementation to meet its stated cost without HECM; P801–803 leaves that check open.

**Literature assessment.**

I found no standalone published theorem stating this exact \(\ell^2nq\) family bound. Its proof does follow from standard published ingredients. Harper’s progression upper bound supplies the substance of DS, including non-coprime residues. [Harper, *Minor arcs, mean values and restriction theory*, Smooth Numbers Result 3](https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/107457/7/WRAP-minor-arcs-mean-values-restriction-theory-exponential-sums-over-smooth-numbers-Harper-2018.pdf)

Harper’s 2012 Theorem 2 is an all-moduli variance estimate; direct positivity does not supply this family’s required multiplicity savings. His 2025 Corollary 1 is likewise an all-moduli variance asymptotic, with \(Q\ge x^{0.51}\). Neither is a direct citation for BDH_fam. [Harper 2012](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992), [Harper 2025](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.19644)

Thus the family-specific assembly may merit presentation, but the ingredients and Lemma W’s use are standard. The search does not certify novelty.

The required fixes are:

1. Restrict coprimality and Corollary W to their valid hypotheses.
2. Define the moment law before rejection and selection; transfer afterward explicitly.
3. Use common \(\ell\)-independent envelopes before invoking BDH_fam.
4. Correct the harmonic sum, floor equalities, normalized-form wording and Prop. 4.2 description.
5. Rewrite Prop. 7.2; narrow Prop. 7.3 and correct its finite-\(k\) cost comparison.
6. Correct the slack qualifications and Pollard–Strassen prose.

**Final verdict on “Theorem LD* for \((a'''_k)\) is proved”: CORRECT WITH FIXES.** The core second-moment argument survives; the listed repairs do not require a new analytic estimate. **Theorem A‴ is a correct conditional reduction** on LV6 for the new design, HECM, and the stated irreducibility transfer. It remains conditional.
