For \(d=3\), this is \(s-\nu+\eta _0>0\); this boundary case also works.

The use of two different multiplicity bounds in D272 is legitimate. On each block,
\[
\nu(m')\le A,\qquad \nu(m')\le B_R
\]
implies
\[
\sum\nu(m')f(m')
 \le \min(A,B_R)\sum f(m')
 \ll A\,X^*\Psi(X^*)+B_RR^2\Psi(X^*).
\]

There are **no omitted denominator classes**. A denominator with positive multiplicity either has no special prime factor and belongs to (A) or (B), or has one uniquely determined special prime to exponent \(1\) or \(2\), belonging to (C). Denominators containing two special primes have multiplicity zero.

Non-reduced classes are included in (A). Negative values are handled by reflection. Zero is explicitly excluded, and \(n=0\) is excluded by the magnitude filter.

